<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Zuhab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-safavid-war-1623-1639">Ottoman–Safavid War (1623–1639)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Zuhab was the peace agreement of 1639 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It ended a long series of wars and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in Ottoman hands. Accounts of the Safavid Empire note its lasting impact, since the frontier it established approximates the modern border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and Iraq.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab.jpg" alt="Treaty of Zuhab" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flag of Ottoman Empire (1517-1793) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ottoman_Empire_(1517-1793).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Safavid-Ottoman rivalry turned the Sunni-Shia divide into a geopolitical fault line, and the repeated wars of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries produced frontiers, most durably that of the <b>Treaty of Zuhab</b> of 1639, whose line broadly anticipates the modern Iran-Iraq border, reflecting confessional as much as strategic logic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
